    },
    {
      "key": "ball_guide_title",
      "value": "Buying guide: what to look for"
    },
    {
      "key": "ball_guide",
      "value": "<b><color=#FF66BF>Hook</color></b>: how far the ball can curve. More hook makes it easier to hit the pocket (between pins 1 and 3) at a good angle for strikes, but it is harder to control.\n<b><color=#4DD9FF>Length</color></b>: how far the ball skids before it starts to turn. Long balls turn late; short balls turn early.\n<b><color=#FFBF40>Backend</color></b>: how sharply it turns at the end. A strong backend enters the pins at a steeper angle and carries more pins; a weak one rolls a smooth arc.\n<b><color=#73FF8C>Control</color></b>: how predictable the ball stays once it picks up lane oil. High control shrugs off oil (and remember to wipe your ball!).\n<b>Coverstock</b>: plastic (spare ball) → urethane (steady) → pearl / hybrid / solid reactive (most hook).\n<b>Core</b>: asymmetric cores flare more and react harder; symmetric cores are smoother.\n<b>Surface</b>: polished skids longer and snaps; sanded grabs early and arcs smoothly.\n<b>Weight</b>: heavier balls hit harder and scatter pins more, but leave the hand a little slower. About 10% of body weight, up to 16 lb, is the usual advice."
    },
